      "description": "Watch River Plate vs Rosario Central highlights and goals from Argentine Primera División on ReFooty: Football Highlights Hub. Match played on August 3, 2026. The Liga Profesional Argentina produced a high-intensity spectacle on August 3, 2026, as River Plate welcomed Rosario Central to a packed stadium. Both sides sought to impose their rhythm in a contest that promised to be decided by the narrowest of margins. Early Intensity and a Costly Mistake The match began with high stakes and physical play, forcing the referee to reach for his pocket early on. Joaquin Freitas was cautioned just 5 minutes in, followed by Lautaro Rivero and Fausto Vera as the atmosphere grew increasingly combative. While both teams struggled to find a clinical edge in the final third, the deadlock was broken by a moment of pure misfortune. In the 27th minute, veteran defender Nicolas Otamendi inadvertently directed the ball into his own net, giving the visitors a lead through a costly own goal. As the first half progressed, the yellow cards continued to pile up. Emanuel Coronel and Lucas Martinez Quarta were both booked, reflecting a game defined more by tactical fouls and defensive pressure than fluid attacking football. By the time the whistle blew for the interval, the tension was palpable across the pitch. Tactical Shifts and Second Half Pressure Seeking a spark, River Plate introduced Tomas Galvan at the start of the second half. The tactical battle intensified as the hosts pushed for an equalizer, while Rosario Central focused on maintaining their defensive shape. A flurry of substitutions followed, with Lucas Beltran and Federico Navarro entering the fray to provide fresh energy. The physical nature of the game remained unchanged, and even the iconic Angel Di Maria found himself in the referee's book for a late challenge in the 65th minute. Late Drama and the Final Stand The closing stages were filled with disciplinary drama that threatened to shift the momentum once again. In the 83rd minute, Emanuel Coronel received his second yellow card and was sent off, leaving his side to defend their narrow lead with only ten men. A series of strategic substitutions followed as both managers adjusted for the final onslaught. Despite the numerical advantage and seven minutes of added time, the defensive line held firm. The final whistle at the 97th minute confirmed a hard-fought result born from defensive resilience and a single, decisive error."
